export default {
  "p2-person-01": "I'd like to talk about my high school English teacher, Mrs Chen, who has probably had the biggest influence on my life. As for who this person is, she was our homeroom teacher for three years and taught us English literature. Speaking of how I know her, she was the first teacher who ever asked me to stay after class — not to punish me, but to talk about the essays I wrote. She was incredibly patient, with a gentle sense of humour, and she treated every student's opinion as if it mattered. In class, she made Shakespeare feel alive, which was no small achievement for a classroom full of restless teenagers. She also encouraged me to keep a journal in English, a habit I still keep to this day. I think the reason she influenced me so deeply is that she saw potential in me before I saw it in myself. When I felt like giving up on my studies, she reminded me that ability is only the starting point, and that consistency matters more. So that's why Mrs Chen remains the person I credit for my love of language and my confidence as a learner.",
  "p2-person-02": "I'd like to talk about my neighbour Mr Okafor, who is hands down the most interesting person on our street. As for who this neighbour is, he is a retired sailor in his seventies who lives alone in the flat opposite mine. Speaking of how I got to know him, we met two years ago when I helped him carry his groceries up the stairs, and since then we have fallen into a routine of chatting on the balcony. What makes him so fascinating is his storehouse of stories — he has sailed across three oceans and can name every port he has ever docked at. He also keeps a tiny herb garden on his windowsill and grows the most fragrant basil I have ever smelled. He once showed me how to tie a sailor's knot using only a shoelace, and he tells vivid tales about storms at sea that sound like scenes from a novel. I find him interesting because he lives so simply yet has experienced so much; every conversation with him leaves me with a new fact or a new way of seeing things. So that's why I genuinely look forward to bumping into Mr Okafor whenever I leave my flat.",
  "p2-place-01": "I'd like to talk about the old book market by the river in my hometown, which is easily my favourite place to visit. As for where this place is, it stretches along the embankment for about two hundred metres, under a row of ancient plane trees. Speaking of who I usually go there with, I mostly go with my best friend, though sometimes I wander there alone. What I do there is browse the second-hand stalls, drink cheap soy milk, and hunt for out-of-print books that you cannot find anywhere online. The sellers are mostly retirees who love nothing more than discussing literature with strangers. I like this place because time seems to slow down there; there is no music blaring, no flashing advertisements, just the sound of pages turning and the river flowing past. Last month I found a 1980s edition of a poetry collection for the equivalent of a dollar, and it is now one of my most treasured possessions. The market reminds me that some of the best things in life are slow, cheap, and impossible to order with one click. So that's why I keep going back, almost every single weekend.",
  "p2-place-02": "I'd like to talk about a small coastal town called Nan'ao, where I spent a long weekend last autumn. As for where this place is, it sits on an island off the coast of Guangdong, surrounded by turquoise water and wind turbines on the hills. Speaking of when I went there, I visited during the National Day holiday with two university friends. What we did there was cycle around the island, eat freshly caught seafood, and simply sit on the beach watching the waves roll in. I remember lying on the sand at dusk, listening to the rhythm of the tide, and feeling every worry drain out of my body. Being near the water made me feel calm in a way that city life never does; there is something about the endless horizon that puts your problems into perspective. We also watched the sunrise from a lighthouse, which was absolutely breathtaking. I enjoyed being near the water because it felt like a reset button for my mind — no notifications, no deadlines, just salt air and the sound of the sea. So that's why that weekend by the water remains one of my favourite memories.",
  "p2-object-01": "I'd like to talk about a fountain pen I own, which may sound ordinary but means the world to me. As for what this thing is, it is a black Parker pen from the 1980s with a worn gold nib and a tiny chip on the cap. Speaking of how long I have had it, my grandfather gave it to me on my fifteenth birthday, so I have owned it for nearly eight years. He used this same pen to sign every contract in his small printing business, and later to write letters to my grandmother. I got it when he decided to retire, and he told me that a good pen should outlive its owner's hands. The pen still writes beautifully; the ink flows smoothly, as if it remembers every word it has ever written. I keep it in a velvet case and only take it out for special occasions, like signing exam papers or writing birthday cards. It is important to me not because of its monetary value — it is probably worth very little — but because it carries my grandfather's warmth and history. Holding it makes me feel connected to him, even though he passed away two years ago. So that's why this pen is my most precious possession.",
  "p2-object-02": "I'd like to talk about a hand-knitted scarf I received from my grandmother last winter. As for what the gift was, it is a thick, cream-coloured scarf with a pattern of small blue diamonds running along the edges. Speaking of who gave it to me, it was my grandmother, who learned to knit from her own mother when she was a girl. I received it in December, right before my final exams, and I remember the look of pride on her face when she handed it to me. At first I felt a little embarrassed, because honestly, most of my friends were giving each other gadgets and headphones. But the moment I wrapped it around my neck, I understood its value; it was warm, sturdy, and clearly made with hours of patient work. I could even see a tiny mistake in one corner, and my grandmother had left it there on purpose, saying it gave the scarf character. That detail touched me more than any expensive present ever could. I felt incredibly grateful, because the gift was not really a scarf — it was her time, her skill, and her love, folded into wool. So that's why receiving that scarf made me feel so special.",
  "p2-experience-01": "I'd like to talk about the day I received my university admission result, which I consider the most important event in my life so far. As for what the event was, it was the moment, in late July, when the online system finally released the admission list for the national entrance exam. Speaking of when it happened, it was around eleven at night, and I had been refreshing the webpage every few minutes for hours. My parents were with me, sitting nervously on the sofa, and my mother kept making cups of tea that nobody drank. When my name finally appeared on the screen, I remember letting out a shout so loud that I probably woke up the neighbours. The three of us hugged in the middle of the living room, and my father, who rarely shows emotion, had tears in his eyes. This event was important because it marked the end of years of grinding study and the beginning of a completely new chapter in a different city. It also proved to me that consistent effort really does pay off, however slow the progress feels. So that's why that single night remains the turning point of my life.",
  "p2-experience-02": "I'd like to talk about a time I helped my classmate Kevin prepare for his maths resit. As for who I helped, Kevin was a quiet guy in my dormitory who had failed the subject twice and was on the verge of giving up. Speaking of why he needed help, he had missed several months of classes because of a serious illness, and the textbook might as well have been written in another language for him. What I did was set up a simple routine: every evening for three weeks, we sat in the library for an hour and a half, and I explained one topic at a time, from basic calculus to probability. The key was that I never let him move on until he could explain the concept back to me in his own words. It was slow going, and there were days when he was clearly frustrated, but we kept at it. When his results came out, he had passed with a comfortable margin, and I honestly felt prouder than I had felt about any of my own grades. After helping him, I realised that teaching is one of the most rewarding things a person can do. So that's why that experience of helping Kevin has stayed with me.",
  "p2-activity-01": "I'd like to talk about photography, which has been my favourite hobby for the past few years. As for what the hobby is, I am not talking about professional studio work — I simply carry a small mirrorless camera and try to capture ordinary moments in interesting ways. Speaking of how I started it, my uncle lent me his old camera during a family trip, and I was instantly hooked by the idea that I could freeze a second of time forever. I do it almost every weekend, usually wandering around the older districts of my city where the light falls beautifully between the buildings. What I love most is street photography, because it forces you to be patient and observant; you wait for the right moment, and sometimes the best shot appears when you least expect it. Photography has also changed how I see the world — I now notice shadows, reflections, and small details that I used to walk past without a glance. It is a hobby that costs little beyond time, yet it rewards me with a visual diary of my life. So that's why photography is the hobby I genuinely cannot imagine giving up.",
  "p2-activity-02": "I'd like to talk about swimming, which is the skill I am most proud of having learned. As for what the skill is, it is the ability to swim freestyle and breaststroke comfortably in open water, not just in a pool. Speaking of how I learned it, I was actually terrified of water as a child, so my father signed me up for lessons at the local sports centre when I was nine. The first few sessions were pure misery — I clung to the edge of the pool and refused to let go. But my coach was incredibly encouraging, and he broke the skill down into tiny steps: first blowing bubbles, then floating, then kicking, until one day I swam a whole length without realising it. It took me nearly a full year to become genuinely confident in deep water. This skill has been useful in ways I never expected; it keeps me fit, it calms my mind after stressful days, and last summer it even helped me rescue a dog that had fallen into a canal. Learning to swim taught me that fear is mostly just unfamiliarity in disguise. So that's why swimming is the skill I value most.",
  "p2-media-01": "I'd like to talk about the film Interstellar, which is the movie I have enjoyed most in recent years. As for what the film was, it is a science fiction epic directed by Christopher Nolan, about a group of astronauts who travel through a wormhole to find a new home for humanity. Speaking of when and where I watched it, I saw it in a cinema on the opening weekend with my cousin, and we both walked out completely speechless. What struck me most was not the special effects, though they were stunning, but the emotional core of the story — a father who has to choose between saving the world and staying with his daughter. There is a scene where he watches decades of his children's messages in a few minutes, and I remember tears running down my face in the dark. The film also made me think about time, love, and how small we are in the universe, yet how powerful human connection can be. I enjoyed it because it managed to be both intellectually exciting and deeply moving at the same time. So that's why Interstellar remains my benchmark for what cinema can achieve.",
  "p2-media-02": "I'd like to talk about Wikipedia, which is honestly the website I use more than any other. As for what the website is, it is the free online encyclopaedia where anyone can look up almost any topic, from quantum physics to the history of dumplings. Speaking of how I found it, I first came across it during secondary school, when a teacher banned us from copying from it but secretly told us it was a great starting point for research. What I use it for is mainly background reading; whenever I hear an unfamiliar term in a podcast or read about a historical figure in the news, I immediately open Wikipedia to get the full picture. I also love its feature of showing related articles, because one click always leads to another, and before I know it, I have spent an hour learning about something completely random. I find it useful because it is fast, free, and surprisingly reliable for general knowledge, as long as you check the sources at the bottom. It has become my intellectual home, in a way. So that's why Wikipedia is the website I could not function without.",
  "p2-work-study-01": "I'd like to talk about a job I would love to do in the future: architect. As for what the job is, an architect designs buildings and spaces, balancing beauty, safety, and practicality on every project. Speaking of what it involves, the work ranges from sketching initial concepts and creating 3D models to visiting construction sites and solving problems on the spot; you spend half your day at a drawing table and the other half in a hard hat. I would like to do it because I have always been fascinated by how a pile of concrete and glass can shape the way people live and feel; a good building can make a street feel welcoming, and a bad one can make it feel cold. To get this job, I would need to complete a five-year architecture degree, build a strong portfolio, and probably work as an assistant for several years before I could call myself a proper architect. I know it is a demanding profession with long hours and tight deadlines, but the idea of seeing my designs standing in the real world, used by real people, is worth every late night. So that's why architecture is my dream career.",
  "p2-work-study-02": "I'd like to talk about Mr Wang, my physics teacher in the final year of high school, who influenced me more than any other teacher. As for who this teacher was, he was a middle-aged man in his fifties with wild grey hair and glasses that were always sliding down his nose. Speaking of what subject he taught, he taught physics, a subject I had always dreaded because it felt like abstract formulas floating in the air. What made him different was his ability to turn every concept into something you could see and touch; he would bring springs, magnets, and even a vacuum pump to class, and he once demonstrated air pressure by crushing a metal can with just boiling water. He also had a habit of asking, 'Why do you think that is?' and would wait in silence, sometimes for a full minute, until someone dared to answer. Because of him, I stopped memorising formulas and started understanding them, and my grades improved dramatically. He influenced me by showing that curiosity, not intelligence, is the real engine of learning. Years later, when I chose a technical field for university, I know his class was the reason. So that's why Mr Wang is the teacher I remember most fondly.",
  "p2-animal-01": "I'd like to talk about elephants, which are my favourite animals in the whole world. As for what the animal is, the elephant is the largest land mammal on Earth, famous for its trunk, its huge ears, and its remarkable intelligence. Speaking of where it lives, wild elephants are found mainly in the forests and savannas of Africa and Asia, though their habitats are shrinking because of human activity. What they look like is hard to describe without sounding poetic: their skin is thick and wrinkled like old tree bark, their eyes are surprisingly gentle, and they move with a quiet, deliberate grace despite weighing several tonnes. I like elephants because they seem to possess a deep emotional intelligence; they mourn their dead, comfort each other when upset, and remember friends and enemies for decades. There is a famous saying that an elephant never forgets, and scientists have confirmed that their memories are extraordinary. Watching a documentary about a rescued elephant calf being reunited with its mother made me cry for the first time in years. I admire how loyal and protective they are as a family. So that's why elephants, more than any other creature, capture my heart.",
  "p2-animal-02": "I'd like to talk about a time I saw wild macaques in their natural environment, which was one of the most magical mornings of my life. As for where I was, it was on a misty mountain in southern China, on a trail that climbed through thick bamboo forest. Speaking of what animals I saw, a whole troop of about thirty macaques appeared on the rocks above us — mothers carrying babies, young males chasing each other, and one old male sitting apart like a king watching his kingdom. What I was doing there was hiking with a small tour group at sunrise, hoping to see the famous sea of clouds. The monkeys, however, stole the show; they groomed each other, swung between branches, and watched us with the same curiosity we felt towards them. Seeing them in the wild, rather than in a cage, made me realise how much their freedom matters; they were not performing for anyone, just living their lives. I felt a strange mix of joy and humility, as if I had been allowed a glimpse into a world that does not revolve around humans. So that's why that encounter with the macaques stays vivid in my memory.",
  "p2-technology-01": "I'd like to talk about my smartwatch, which has quietly become the piece of technology I cannot live without. As for what it is, it is a small wearable device that tracks my heart rate, sleep, and exercise, and also shows me messages without needing to pull out my phone. Speaking of how I use it, I wear it from the moment I wake up to the moment I go to bed; it reminds me to stand up when I have been sitting too long, counts my steps, and even monitors my blood oxygen level when I feel unwell. I have had it for about two years, ever since my brother gave it to me as a birthday present. It is important to me because it has genuinely changed my habits; seeing my step count and sleep data every day made me take my health seriously for the first time. I now walk at least ten thousand steps daily, and I go to bed earlier because the watch shows me how poorly I sleep after late nights. It is a small screen on my wrist, but it holds me accountable in a way no diary ever could. So that's why my smartwatch is the technology I rely on most.",
  "p2-technology-02": "I'd like to talk about a note-taking app called Obsidian, which has completely changed how I study. As for what the app is, it is a tool for writing and connecting notes, where every idea you record can be linked to related ideas, forming a kind of personal knowledge web. Speaking of how I discovered it, I came across it on a YouTube video about study techniques, and I was sceptical at first because it looked complicated. What I use it for is organising my language learning — I keep vocabulary lists, grammar rules, and example sentences in it, and I link them together so that every new word connects to older ones. I find it useful because normal notebooks bury information; Obsidian lets me see the connections, and those connections are what actually help me remember things. There is something satisfying about watching my knowledge grow like a spider's web, with new strands appearing every day. It is free, works offline, and never loses my data. Using it for a year has improved my retention more than any other method I have tried. So that's why Obsidian is the app I recommend to every student I meet.",
  "p2-food-01": "I'd like to talk about a hotpot dinner I had with my university friends, which remains the most enjoyable meal of my life. As for what the meal was, it was a classic Sichuan hotpot — a bubbling pot of spicy red broth in the middle of the table, surrounded by plates of sliced beef, mushrooms, tofu, and fresh vegetables. Speaking of where I had it, it was at a small, crowded restaurant near our campus that was famous for its fiery broth and its generous portions. What made the evening special was the company; it was the last dinner before we all graduated and scattered to different cities, so the mood was equal parts laughter and sadness. We dipped ingredients into the pot, competed over who could eat the spiciest piece of beef, and somehow turned a simple meal into a four-hour marathon of stories and toasts. I enjoyed it because hotpot is the most social food there is — everyone cooks together, shares together, and burns their tongues together. That night, surrounded by steam and laughter, I felt the warmth of friendship more than I ever had before. So that's why that hotpot dinner is my favourite meal memory.",
  "p2-food-02": "I'd like to talk about a small noodle shop near my office that has become my favourite restaurant. As for where this restaurant is, it is tucked away in a narrow lane about a five-minute walk from my workplace, with no signboard other than a faded yellow flag. Speaking of what kind of food it serves, it serves hand-pulled noodles, made fresh every morning by the owner, who pulls the dough with such skill that it looks like a performance. The menu is tiny — maybe eight dishes — but everything is cooked to order, and the broth simmers for hours until it is rich and golden. I go there at least twice a week, usually for lunch, and the owner now greets me by name and remembers that I like extra coriander. I like this restaurant because it has something that big chains can never replicate: character. The chairs wobble, the walls are covered in customers' scribbles, and the owner chats with everyone as if they were old friends. Eating there feels like being part of a small community rather than a transaction. For the price of a bowl of noodles, I get warmth, flavour, and a sense of belonging. So that's why that little noodle shop is my favourite place to eat.",
  "p2-festival-01": "I'd like to talk about the Spring Festival, which is the most important festival in my country and the one I celebrate with the most joy. As for what the festival is, it is the Chinese New Year, marking the beginning of the lunar calendar, and it usually falls in January or February. Speaking of when it takes place, celebrations begin on New Year's Eve and officially last about a week, though the whole season feels festive for much longer. How people celebrate it is with a flurry of traditions: families clean their homes to sweep away bad luck, paste red couplets on their doors, set off firecrackers, and gather for a lavish reunion dinner. Children receive red envelopes containing money, which is my favourite tradition even now that I am an adult. The festival is important to me because it is the one time of year when my whole extended family — uncles, aunts, cousins, grandparents — squeezes into one house, cooks together, plays cards until midnight, and argues happily about everything. In an age where everyone is busy and scattered, the Spring Festival is a stubborn, wonderful reminder of where I come from. So that's why this festival means so much to me.",
  "p2-festival-02": "I'd like to talk about the tradition of making dumplings together at the Winter Solstice, which families in northern China follow every December. As for what the tradition is, on the shortest day of the year, the whole family gathers in the kitchen to wrap dumplings — filling thin rounds of dough with pork, cabbage, and chives, then pinching the edges shut into little crescent shapes. Speaking of when people follow it, it happens on the solstice itself, around the twenty-first of December, and it is said that eating dumplings on this day keeps your ears from freezing in winter. How it is carried out is quite ritualistic: everyone has a role, from rolling the dough to passing the wrappers, and the oldest family member usually makes the first dumpling. There is also a playful tradition of hiding a coin in one dumpling, and whoever finds it is said to have good luck for the coming year. This tradition is important because it turns a simple meal into an occasion for togetherness; in my memory, the kitchen is always steamy, noisy, and full of laughter. It reminds us that some happiness is handmade, quite literally. So that's why the dumpling tradition matters so much to our family.",
  "p2-transport-01": "I'd like to talk about an overnight train journey I made from my hometown to Beijing, which was my longest trip by public transport. As for where I went, I travelled from a small city in the south to the capital, a distance of over a thousand kilometres. Speaking of what kind of public transport I used, it was a sleeper train — the kind with six bunks in each compartment, where you spend the night lying on a thin mattress while the landscape rolls past the window. I was with my mother, who was taking me to the city for my university entrance interview. What I remember most is the rhythm of the journey: the clickety-clack of the wheels, the conductor's announcements in the early morning, and the smell of instant noodles drifting through the carriage. We shared our compartment with a retired couple who told us stories about their travels and insisted on sharing their boiled eggs. I felt a strange mix of excitement and nervousness the whole way, watching the fields turn into factories and then into skyscrapers. The journey took fourteen hours, yet it felt like a gentle bridge between my old life and the new one waiting for me. So that's why that train journey stays in my memory.",
  "p2-transport-02": "I'd like to talk about my daily commute, which is both the most tedious and the most predictable part of my day. As for how I travel, I take the metro for about forty minutes each way, changing lines once in the middle. Speaking of how long it takes, door to door, it is roughly fifty minutes in the morning and a bit longer in the evening when the trains are packed like sardines. What I do during the journey is try to make the time useful: I usually listen to English podcasts on my headphones, review vocabulary on my phone, or simply people-watch when my brain is too tired to focus. Sometimes I read e-books, and I have finished more novels during commutes than in any other setting. Honestly, my feelings about the commute are mixed; on the one hand, it eats nearly two hours of my day, and the rush-hour crowds can be truly exhausting. On the other hand, that forced downtime has become a kind of daily ritual — a buffer between work and home where nobody can reach me. I have learned to treat the commute as my own quiet bubble rather than wasted time. So that's why, despite its frustrations, I have made peace with my daily commute.",
  "p2-health-01": "I'd like to talk about running, which is the main thing I do to stay healthy. As for what I do, I go for a run three or four times a week, usually in the park near my home, covering about five kilometres each time. Speaking of when I started doing it, I began two years ago, after my doctor told me my cholesterol was slightly high and that my sedentary lifestyle was catching up with me. The first month was brutal — I could barely manage two laps without gasping for air — but I kept at it, adding a little distance each week. How often I do it varies with the season; in summer I run early in the morning before the heat, and in winter I switch to the evening. Running helps me stay healthy in obvious ways: my stamina has improved, my blood test results are back to normal, and I sleep much more deeply. But the mental benefits are just as important; there is something meditative about the rhythm of my footsteps, and I have solved more problems in my head while running than I ever have at a desk. It has become my anchor, the one constant in my week. So that's why running is my go-to way of staying healthy.",
  "p2-health-02": "I'd like to talk about badminton, which is the exercise I do most regularly and enjoy the most. As for what the exercise is, it is a fast racket sport played with a shuttlecock, where two or four players hit it back and forth across a net. Speaking of where I do it, I play at the community sports centre near my apartment, which has proper courts with good lighting, and I book a court every Sunday morning. How often I do it is pretty consistent — once a week with a group of friends, though sometimes we squeeze in an extra session on weekdays when everyone is free. I enjoy badminton because it does not feel like exercise at all; it feels like play. The rallies are quick and explosive, and there is a wonderful moment of satisfaction when you smash the shuttlecock so hard that your opponent cannot even react. It also exercises my whole body — my legs, my shoulders, and my reaction speed — while the social side keeps me coming back; we laugh, we complain about our joints, and we go for noodles afterwards. Badminton has become my weekly highlight, the hour when I forget about work completely. So that's why badminton is my favourite form of exercise.",
  "p2-shopping-01": "I'd like to talk about a pair of hiking boots I bought two years ago, which is probably the best money I have ever spent. As for what I bought, they are sturdy leather boots with waterproof lining and deep grip soles, made by a well-known outdoor brand. Speaking of where I bought them, I got them at an outdoor equipment store during a winter sale, and they cost me the equivalent of about a hundred and fifty dollars — not cheap, but far less than the original price. I decided to invest in them after a hiking trip where my flimsy trainers fell apart halfway up the mountain. Why I think they were worth every cent is simple: I have worn them on at least thirty hikes since, in rain, snow, and rocky terrain, and they have never let me down. My feet stay dry and blister-free even after ten-hour days on the trail. In fact, the boots have saved me money in the long run, because I no longer buy cheap replacements that wear out every few months. They are scuffed and muddy now, but they fit my feet like a second skin. So that's why those hiking boots were absolutely worth the money.",
  "p2-shopping-02": "I'd like to talk about a time I saved money to buy my first proper camera, which taught me more about discipline than any class ever did. As for what I was saving for, it was a mirrorless camera that cost about eight hundred dollars, far beyond what my student budget could cover. Speaking of how I managed to save, I used a combination of small sacrifices: I gave up buying coffee and snacks at the campus café, cooked all my meals at home, took on a part-time tutoring job on weekends, and set aside a fixed amount every single month, no matter what. The hardest part was watching my friends eat out and travel while I stayed in, but I kept a photo of the camera on my phone wallpaper as motivation. It took me eleven months to reach my goal, and when I finally transferred the money and walked out of the shop with the camera in my hands, I felt a kind of quiet joy that instant gratification has never given me. That camera has taken thousands of photos since, and every time I use it, I remember the patient months behind it. So that's why that period of saving money was so meaningful to me.",
  "p2-childhood-01": "I'd like to talk about a happy memory from my childhood: the summer holidays I spent at my grandparents' village. As for what the memory was, it is not a single event but a golden collection of moments — waking up to the crowing of roosters, eating breakfast on the stone steps, and chasing dragonflies by the stream. Speaking of when it happened, I spent every July and August there between the ages of about seven and twelve. Who was with me was my older brother and my two cousins, and together we formed a little gang that the whole village soon knew about. We climbed mulberry trees until our fingers were purple, caught tadpoles in jam jars, and played hide-and-seek until our grandmother called us home for dinner by yelling from the doorway. Why this memory is special to me is hard to put into words; it was a time when life was unbelievably simple, with no homework, no phones, and no worries beyond whether the ice pops would melt before we finished them. I think it shaped my belief that happiness does not need to be expensive or complicated. So that's why those village summers remain the happiest memories of my childhood.",
  "p2-childhood-02": "I'd like to talk about a game I played endlessly as a child: jumping elastic bands, which we called 'rubber band jumping'. As for what the game was, two children would stretch a long loop of elastic between their ankles, and a third child would perform a series of jumps, kicks, and twists over and under it, without ever touching the band with anything but her feet. Speaking of where I played it, we played in the school playground and in the narrow alleyways near my home, wherever the ground was flat enough. Who I played it with was my classmates, mostly girls, though the boys occasionally joined in to show off. The game had many levels, and the band moved higher each round — from ankles to knees to waist — until the jumps became almost acrobatic. I enjoyed it because it was competitive yet friendly; we cheered for each other and invented new moves together, and there was a real sense of achievement when you cleared the highest level. It also kept us outdoors for hours, rain or shine. Looking back, that simple loop of elastic gave us endless entertainment without a single screen. So that's why rubber band jumping is the childhood game I remember most fondly.",
  "p2-communication-01": "I'd like to talk about a conversation I had with my grandfather on the rooftop of our old house, which I still think about years later. As for who I had the conversation with, it was with my grandfather, who was then in his eighties and full of stories he rarely told. Where and when it happened was on a summer evening, about six years ago, when the two of us went up to the roof to escape the heat and watch the stars. What we talked about started with my questions about his youth — he had grown up in poverty during a difficult period — and slowly turned into him telling me about the jobs he had done: farmhand, factory worker, street vendor, and finally a small shop owner. He told me he had never once chosen a job he loved; he chose jobs that fed his family, and he told me that loving your work is a luxury, but doing your work honestly is a dignity. I remember the silence after he said that, with the crickets loud around us. That conversation was memorable because it gave me a completely new perspective on effort and sacrifice, and it made me hug him a little longer the next morning. So that's why that rooftop talk has stayed with me.",
  "p2-communication-02": "I'd like to talk about a time I disagreed with my older brother, and the disagreement was surprisingly serious: whether our family should adopt a stray dog. As for who I disagreed with, it was my brother, who is usually the most easygoing person I know. What we disagreed about was a small, skinny dog that had followed me home from the park one rainy evening; I wanted to keep it, while my brother insisted we could not handle the responsibility, since our parents worked long hours and we were both students. How we both reacted was not pretty at first — we argued loudly in the kitchen, and I accused him of being cold-hearted, while he accused me of being impulsive. For three days we barely spoke, and the dog stayed in a neighbour's yard. The resolution came when my brother secretly built a simple doghouse and put it by our back door; he told me he had been thinking about it, and that he was actually worried about who would care for the animal when I went away to university. We agreed on a schedule of shared duties, and the dog, whom we named Mochi, has been with us ever since. So that's why that disagreement, though painful, ended up bringing us closer.",
  "p2-advice-01": "I'd like to talk about a piece of advice my father gave me when I was about to enter university, which has guided me ever since. As for who gave me the advice, it was my father, a man who speaks little but says a lot when he does. What the advice was is simple: he told me, 'Never spend money you do not have, and never promise more than you can deliver.' I received it during a long walk we took the night before I left for university, when I was excitedly planning all the things I would buy with my first student loan. He listened, then gave me that short sentence, and explained that debt and empty promises are the two traps that have ruined the most lives he knows. At the time I thought the advice was almost too obvious to count as wisdom. But over the years, it has influenced me enormously; I have never owned a credit card with an unpaid balance, I plan my purchases weeks in advance, and I have learned to say 'no' honestly instead of 'yes' weakly. That single sentence has quietly shaped how I handle money and friendships alike. So that's why my father's advice is the one I repeat to myself most often.",
  "p2-advice-02": "I'd like to talk about a piece of advice I gave to my younger cousin, who was terrified about her first job interview. As for who I gave the advice to, it was my cousin Lin, a bright but very shy girl who had just graduated and had an interview at a marketing firm. What the advice was, I remember telling her, 'The interviewer is not testing whether you are perfect; they are testing whether you are easy to work with, so stop trying to impress and start trying to connect.' She needed it because she had been rehearsing robotic answers until two in the morning, and her confidence was crumbling; she kept asking me what the 'correct' answer was to every possible question. I also told her to prepare three stories about real things she had done, and to ask the interviewer questions too, because interviews are a two-way street. Whether it turned out to be helpful — well, she got the job, and she later told me that my advice calmed her down more than anything else she had read. She said that in the interview she actually relaxed, laughed, and treated it like a conversation, exactly as I had suggested. So that's why I am quietly proud of the advice I gave my cousin.",
  "p2-change-01": "I'd like to talk about a change that dramatically improved my life: I started waking up at six o'clock every morning. As for what the change was, it was replacing my chaotic late-night routine with a strict early-bird schedule, going to bed by eleven and rising at dawn. Speaking of when and why I made it, I made it about eighteen months ago, when I realised I was constantly tired, always rushing, and somehow never getting anything done despite working long hours. How I felt about it at first was genuinely miserable; for the first two weeks, the alarm felt like an insult, and I often pressed snooze four or five times. But I forced myself out of bed anyway, and after about a month, my body adjusted. How this change has improved my life is beyond what I expected: I now have two quiet hours every morning to exercise, read, and plan my day before anyone else is awake. I arrive at work calm instead of breathless, and my evenings are free because my work is already under control. Friends joke that I have become a different person, and honestly, I have. That single change gave me back time, energy, and a sense of control over my own day. So that's why waking up early is the change that improved my life most.",
  "p2-change-02": "I'd like to talk about a big decision I made: leaving my hometown to take a job in a city six hundred kilometres away. As for what the decision was, it was choosing a position at a company in the capital, even though it meant leaving my family, my friends, and everything familiar behind. When I made it was two years ago, right after I finished university, and I spent the whole summer going back and forth about it. Who I discussed it with was mainly my parents and my best friend; my mother cried, my father gave me his quiet blessing, and my best friend told me plainly that if I stayed out of fear, I would regret it for the rest of my life. How it has affected my life is hard to overstate — in the new city I have grown up faster than I ever could have at home. I learned to cook, to manage money, to deal with loneliness, and to build a new circle of friends from zero. Professionally, the job opened doors I did not even know existed, and I now earn double what I would have earned at home. The decision was terrifying at the time, but it turned out to be the best one I have ever made. So that's why leaving home was my most significant decision.",
  "p2-team-01": "I'd like to talk about a team project I worked on in my final year of university: organising a charity fundraising event for a local animal shelter. As for what the project was, it was a weekend street fair with food stalls, a flea market, and a small concert, with all profits going to the shelter. Who I worked with was a team of eight classmates, and we had exactly two months to plan everything from scratch. What my role was, I was in charge of publicity — I designed posters, ran the social media accounts, and somehow persuaded a local radio station to mention our event. The team worked together remarkably well, mainly because we had clear roles and a shared deadline; the design people designed, the finance people tracked every cent, and we all met twice a week to report progress. Of course, there were disagreements — I remember a heated argument over the poster colours — but we always resolved them by voting. On the day itself, it rained in the morning, and for two panicked hours we thought the event would fail; then the sun came out, and we ended up raising almost double our target. That project taught me that a good team multiplies individual effort. So that's why working on that team project is one of my proudest experiences.",
  "p2-team-02": "I'd like to talk about a time I worked with a group of friends to achieve something surprisingly challenging: throwing a surprise birthday party for our best friend Anna. As for what we were trying to achieve, we wanted to organise a party she would never forget — a rooftop dinner with fairy lights, a homemade cake, and a video montage of her whole life, all without her finding out. Who I worked with was a team of six friends, and we had three weeks to pull it off. How we divided the work was quite natural: two people handled the food, two took charge of the decorations, one secretly collected photos and videos, and I was the 'distraction officer', responsible for keeping Anna away from the apartment on the day. The plan nearly collapsed twice — the cake collapsed in the oven, and Anna almost came home early to fetch a charger. But everyone improvised, and when she finally walked onto the roof, she burst into tears, and so did half of us. What I felt about working with others was pure warmth; the moment belonged to everyone who had contributed, and I realised that shared effort creates a bond that solo success never can. So that's why that surprise party is my favourite memory of teamwork.",
  "p2-skill-01": "I'd like to talk about a skill I am learning right now: sign language. As for what the skill is, I am learning Chinese Sign Language, the system used by deaf communities in my country, which communicates through hand shapes, movements, and facial expressions. Why I decided to learn it is a bit personal — last year, a deaf colleague joined our office, and I watched how isolated she looked during meetings, even though everyone was friendly. I wanted to at least greet her properly in her own language. How I am learning it is through an online course, plus weekly practice sessions with my colleague herself, who is unbelievably patient and laughs at my clumsy hand movements. I practise for about twenty minutes every evening, repeating the signs in front of a mirror, and I keep a notebook of new vocabulary. How I feel about my progress is honestly mixed; I can now hold a basic conversation and understand about half of what she signs at normal speed, which feels wonderful, but the grammar is so different from spoken language that I often get tangled. Still, the first time she signed 'thank you, friend' to me, I nearly cried with happiness. So that's why learning sign language has become my favourite new skill.",
  "p2-skill-02": "I'd like to talk about something I learned from a family member: I learned how to repair bicycles from my father. As for what I learned, it was not just the mechanics — how to patch a punctured inner tube, adjust the brakes, and true a wobbly wheel — but also a whole way of approaching problems. Which family member taught me was my father, a mechanic who worked in a small repair shop all his life. How he taught it to me was never with formal lessons; instead, when I was about ten and my bike chain kept slipping, he simply handed me the tools and said, 'Take it apart and see.' He would let me struggle with a part for half an hour before quietly showing me the trick, and he always insisted that I understand why a repair worked, not just how. Why this skill is valuable to me goes beyond saving money on repairs; it gave me a mindset of patient curiosity. Whenever I face a broken thing — a laptop, a friendship, a plan — I hear his voice telling me to take it apart gently and look. That lesson, more than the mechanics, has shaped how I live. So that's why the repair skills my father taught me are so precious.",
  "p2-adventure-01": "I'd like to talk about a risky thing I did: bungee jumping from a bridge that hung forty metres above a river. As for what I did, it was a controlled free fall attached to an elastic cord, plummeting towards the water and then bouncing back up like a yo-yo. Why I decided to do it was partly to prove something to myself — I had always thought of myself as a cautious person, and my friends teased that I would chicken out at the last second. How I prepared for it was fairly minimal but honest: I checked that the company had proper safety certificates, I watched three other people jump first, and I spent the whole morning telling myself not to look down. How I felt before, during, and after was a wild rollercoaster of emotions. Before, I was terrified — my heart hammered so loudly I could hear it. During the fall itself, which lasted barely three seconds, my mind went completely blank, and it was strangely peaceful. After, standing on solid ground again, I felt an incredible rush of relief and pride; I had done the thing I feared most, and the world had not ended. It taught me that courage is not the absence of fear but action in spite of it. So that's why that jump remains my riskiest and most rewarding experience.",
  "p2-adventure-02": "I'd like to talk about an exciting experience I had: a hot air balloon ride over a river valley at sunrise. As for what the experience was, it was an early-morning flight in a huge balloon, drifting silently above fields, forests, and a winding river, with the mountains glowing pink in the first light. When and where it happened was last spring, in a tourist region known for its karst peaks and morning mist. Who was with me was my younger sister, who had saved up for months to give me this ride for my birthday. The most exciting part was the moment of take-off, when the burners roared and the ground suddenly dropped away beneath us; my stomach did a little flip, and my sister grabbed my arm and laughed. Then came the strange silence of floating — the only sound was the occasional blast of the burner, and far below, dogs barking and roosters crowing as the valley woke up. We drifted for about an hour, watching the shadows of our balloon glide across the fields, and landed softly in a farmer's meadow, where the farmer waved and offered us tea. I found it exciting because it made the world look small and gentle and full of wonder. So that's why that balloon ride is my most exciting memory.",
  "p2-thoughtfilm-01": "I'd like to talk about a film that made me think deeply: The Truman Show, which I watched for the first time a few years ago. As for what the film was, it is a drama starring Jim Carrey about a man whose entire life — his town, his wife, his best friend — turns out to be a giant television set, with every moment of his existence broadcast to the world. When and where I watched it was late one night at home, alone, with my laptop on my knees and the lights off. What the film was about, in essence, is the search for truth: Truman slowly notices cracks in his perfect world, and he finally sails a small boat across a fake sea to reach a door in the sky. What I thought about after watching it was quite unsettling: how much of my own life is genuinely mine, and how much is shaped by algorithms, advertisements, and other people's expectations? The film made me wonder whether I would have the courage to walk through the door of my own 'set', and what I would find on the other side. I also reflected on how we willingly hand our attention to platforms that watch us just as closely as the audience watched Truman. So that's why The Truman Show is the film that made me think the most.",
  "p2-thoughtfilm-02": "I'd like to talk about a documentary I watched: Blue Planet II, the famous nature series about the world's oceans. As for what the documentary was about, it explored the deep sea, from coral reefs teeming with colour to the pitch-black abyss where bizarre creatures glow in the dark, filmed over four years by underwater camera teams. Where I watched it was at home, on the living room television, over several weekend evenings with my family. What I learned from it was astonishing — that octopuses can change colour and texture in an instant, that a species of fish builds underwater gardens to farm its food, and that some whales sing songs that travel across entire oceans. But the most important lesson came in the final episode, which showed how plastic waste is choking these creatures, from sea birds feeding plastic to their chicks to turtles tangled in fishing nets. I found the documentary interesting not only because the footage was breathtaking, but because it quietly asked me a question: what am I willing to change? After watching it, my family and I started carrying reusable bags and water bottles everywhere, and I stopped buying single-use plastic when I can avoid it. So that's why that documentary, both beautiful and troubling, has stayed with me.",
  "p2-moment-01": "I'd like to talk about a time I felt truly proud of myself: the day I finished my first half marathon. As for what I did, I ran twenty-one kilometres through the streets of my city, which took me two hours and nineteen minutes — a modest time, but a monumental achievement for someone who had never run more than five kilometres before. When and where it happened was last autumn, at the city marathon, which started near the old stadium and wound past all the famous landmarks. Who was there to see it was my entire family, who had come to cheer for me; my mother held a sign that read 'The Slowest Runner Ever, Still Running', and my little nephew ran the last hundred metres beside me, holding my hand. Why I felt so proud is that I had trained for six months, running through rain, heat, and mornings when every muscle screamed to stop. Crossing the finish line, I felt my eyes sting with tears that I could not explain; it was not about the medal, but about the fact that I had kept a promise I had made to myself. That day taught me that pride comes not from winning, but from not quitting. So that's why finishing that half marathon is the moment I am proudest of.",
  "p2-moment-02": "I'd like to talk about a goal I achieved that still makes me smile: learning to play my favourite song on the guitar. As for what the goal was, it was to play the complete version of a well-known ballad — every chord, every strumming pattern, every finger-picked intro — without stopping or making a mistake. How long it took to achieve was about five months, which felt like an eternity when I started and could barely hold the guitar properly. What I did to achieve it was painfully methodical: I practised for half an hour every evening, starting with the chords one by one, then the transitions, then the rhythm, and finally the full song at half speed. I recorded myself every week and cringed at the early videos, but those recordings showed me how far I had come. The worst part was a two-week plateau where I kept stumbling at the same chord change; I nearly gave up, but I slowed the tempo down to almost nothing and rebuilt it from there. How I felt when I finally reached it — I played the whole song through for my roommate, and when I hit the final chord cleanly, we both cheered like idiots. That feeling of earned achievement is addictive. So that's why that goal means so much to me.",
  "p2-quietplace-01": "I'd like to talk about a quiet place I love to go: the reading room of an old library in the centre of my city. As for where this place is, it is on the third floor of a century-old building with high ceilings, wooden reading tables, and windows that overlook a line of ginkgo trees. Speaking of how I found it, I discovered it by accident during my first week in the city, when I was looking for a place to escape the noise of my shared apartment, and I have been going there ever since. What I do there is read, write in my journal, or simply sit and let my thoughts settle; sometimes I do not even open a book — I just watch the dust float in the sunlight and listen to the pages turning around me. Why I like this quiet place is that it feels like a bubble outside of time; in a city where everyone is rushing and shouting into phones, the reading room demands a certain reverence. The elderly librarian nods at me now when I walk in, and there is one seat by the window that everyone seems to know is mine. Going there resets my mind in a way that coffee shops never can. So that's why the library reading room is my favourite quiet place.",
  "p2-quietplace-02": "I'd like to talk about a place where I truly relax: a small park near my home, built around a lotus pond. As for where this place is, it is a ten-minute walk from my apartment, a modest patch of green tucked between two busy roads, with willow trees leaning over the water and a single wooden pavilion in the middle. When I usually go there is in the early evening, right after work, when the crowds have thinned and the streetlights are just beginning to glow. What I do to relax there is simple: I buy a cup of tea from the stall at the gate, walk one full loop around the pond, then sit in the pavilion and watch the fish rise to the surface and the dragonflies skim the water. I sometimes bring a book, but more often I just sit with my own thoughts, letting the day's noise drain out of me. Why this place helps me relax is that it contains no screens, no demands, and no deadlines; the pond has been there for decades, and it will be there tomorrow, which is strangely comforting. Half an hour there makes the rest of my evening feel spacious. So that's why that little park is my refuge.",
  "p2-happiness-01": "I'd like to talk about something small that made me very happy recently: an unexpected video call from my best friend, who has been living abroad for two years. As for what it was, it was a late-night call that arrived while I was sitting at my desk feeling tired and slightly low after a long week. When it happened was about two weeks ago, on a Thursday night, well past the time when either of us would normally call. Who was involved was just the two of us — my friend, who had returned home for a short visit and surprised me by calling instead of messaging, and me, in my pyjamas with a cup of cold tea. We talked for almost two hours about nothing and everything: the ridiculous things our colleagues said, the food we missed from home, and the plans we had quietly given up on. Why it made me so happy is that my friend had remembered that I had been going through a stressful period, and had arranged the call with a gift — she had also posted me a small packet of sweets from her city, which arrived three days later. That combination of being remembered, being listened to, and being surprised filled me with a warmth that lasted for days. Sometimes happiness is simply someone proving that you matter to them. So that's why that phone call made me so happy.",
  "p2-happiness-02": "I'd like to talk about a small thing that reliably makes me happy: my morning coffee ritual. As for what this small thing is, it is the whole little ceremony of grinding fresh beans, boiling water, and slowly pouring it over the grounds in my old ceramic dripper, watching the coffee bloom and drip through. How often I do it is every single morning, without exception — even on days when I am running late, I will make the coffee and carry it out the door with me. Where I usually do it is in my small kitchen, with the window open if the weather allows, listening to a podcast or just enjoying the silence before the day begins. Why such a small thing brings me happiness is hard to explain rationally; I think it is because it is the one part of my day that belongs entirely to me, unhurried and unshared. In a life full of meetings, messages, and obligations, those ten minutes of warmth and aroma feel like a private ritual that no deadline can touch. The smell alone can lift my mood on the gloomiest mornings, and somehow a properly brewed cup makes the whole day feel more manageable. So that's why my morning coffee is the small thing that makes me happiest.",
  "p2-volunteering-01": "I'd like to talk about a time I helped in my community: I volunteered to teach elderly residents how to use smartphones. As for what I did, I joined a weekly programme at the community centre where students like me sat down one-on-one with seniors and walked them through the basics — making video calls, paying utility bills online, booking hospital appointments, and above all, avoiding scam messages. Who I helped were mostly retired people in their sixties and seventies, many of whom had been given phones by their children but were too embarrassed to ask how they worked. Why I decided to help was that my own grandmother had once been cheated by a fake 'bank' call, and I could not stop thinking that a little education might have prevented it. How this experience affected me was profound; the seniors were the most grateful students I have ever met, and one old lady brought me home-made dumplings to say thank you. I also learned patience, because explaining the same step for the tenth time with a smile is genuinely a skill. Seeing an elderly man video-call his granddaughter for the first time and burst into tears of joy — that image will stay with me forever. So that's why that volunteering experience changed how I see my community.",
  "p2-volunteering-02": "I'd like to talk about a volunteering experience I had at an animal shelter, which taught me more than any classroom ever did. As for what the volunteering involved, I spent my summer holiday helping out at a shelter that housed abandoned dogs and cats; my tasks included cleaning the kennels, preparing food, walking the dogs, and, most importantly, socialising the animals so that they could be adopted more easily. Where and when it took place was at a shelter on the edge of my city, from early July to the end of August, four days a week. Who I volunteered with was a small group of students and two full-time staff, plus a retired veterinarian who taught us how to spot signs of illness. What I gained from the experience was immense: I learned practical skills like giving medication and reading a dog's body language, but more than that, I learned about patience and quiet love. The hardest part was the day one of our dogs — a gentle brown mutt we had named Biscuit — was adopted; I cried, and so did the staff, but they were happy tears. Seeing shy animals slowly trust again reminded me that kindness is a form of skill that needs practice. So that's why that summer at the shelter was my most meaningful volunteering experience.",
  "p2-dreamhome-01": "I'd like to talk about my dream home, which I have pictured in my mind since I was a teenager. As for where it would be, it would be in an old part of a medium-sized city, perhaps a quiet lane lined with plane trees, far enough from the centre to be peaceful but close enough to walk to a good market. What it would look like would be a renovated courtyard house — the traditional single-storey style with rooms arranged around a central open yard, its grey brick walls softened by climbing roses. What special features it would have is where my imagination really runs wild: a small vegetable garden in the courtyard, a roof terrace with a hammock where I could read in summer, a wall of bookshelves in the main room, and a big wooden kitchen table where friends could gather. There would also be a workshop corner where I could tinker with repairs, and perhaps a little room for writing with a window facing the courtyard. Why this would be my dream home is that it combines everything I value: nature, quiet, creativity, and hospitality. It would be a home that grows old with me, full of scratches and stories. So that's why a courtyard house with a garden is my dream home.",
  "p2-dreamhome-02": "I'd like to talk about a place I would love to live in: a small town in the mountains of Yunnan province, in the southwest of China. As for where this place is, it is a historic town built beside a lake, with snow-capped mountains rising behind it and old stone streets lined with traditional buildings and flowering trees. What I know about it comes mostly from a trip I took there two years ago; I know the weather is mild all year, the air is clean, the locals are famously laid-back, and the food is fresh and vegetable-heavy, with wild mushrooms as a local specialty. Why I would like to live there is that it offers the opposite of my current life: instead of rush-hour crowds and screens, there would be mountains to hike on weekends, a lake to walk around every evening, and a pace of life that does not measure worth in how busy you are. How my life would be different there is easy to imagine — I would wake to birdsong instead of traffic, know my neighbours by name, eat dinner before eight o'clock, and probably sleep better than I have in years. It might not have the career opportunities of a big city, but it would have something more precious: time. So that's why that mountain town is where I dream of living.",
  "p2-holiday-01": "I'd like to talk about a holiday I truly enjoyed: a week I spent in Japan with my best friend, two springs ago. As for where we went, we flew to Tokyo first, then took the bullet train to Kyoto, and finished with a day at the seaside in Kamakura. Who I went with was my best friend, who is the perfect travel companion because we want the same things — long walks, cheap food, and no rigid schedules. What we did there was wonderfully simple: we wandered through temples and bamboo forests, ate ramen in tiny shops where the counter had only six seats, got lost in narrow alleys, and sat by the river in Kyoto watching the cherry blossoms drift down like snow. One evening we took a night bus to a mountain village to see the stars, and we both cried a little, though neither of us would admit it. Why I enjoyed this holiday so much is that it had the perfect balance of discovery and rest; every day brought something new, yet we never felt rushed. Travelling with someone who laughs at the same things as you doubles the joy of everything. That week recharged me for an entire year. So that's why that trip to Japan remains my favourite holiday.",
  "p2-holiday-02": "I'd like to talk about a short break I took last winter: a three-day weekend at a hot spring resort in the mountains, about three hours' drive from my city. As for where we went, it was a small, old-fashioned hot spring town built along a river, where the guesthouses have outdoor pools fed by natural thermal water and the air smells of sulphur and pine. How long the break was, as I said, just three days — from Friday evening to Monday morning — which was exactly the recharge I needed after a heavy work month. What I did during it was gloriously little: I soaked in the outdoor pool under the stars, hiked along a forest trail to a waterfall, ate a seven-course dinner of local mountain vegetables, and slept nine hours every night. I also spent one whole morning doing absolutely nothing but sitting on the balcony watching the steam rise off the river. How the break made me feel afterwards was remarkable; when I returned to work on Tuesday, my colleagues kept asking if I had taken two weeks off, because I looked so rested. That short break proved to me that you do not need a long holiday to reset — sometimes three days in the right place is enough. So that's why that hot spring weekend was the perfect short break.",
  "p2-celebrity-01": "I'd like to talk about a famous person I deeply admire: Tu Youyou, the Chinese pharmaceutical chemist who won the Nobel Prize in Medicine. As for who this person is, she is a scientist in her nineties who spent decades researching traditional Chinese medicine, and in the 1970s she led the team that discovered artemisinin, a compound derived from sweet wormwood that has saved millions of lives from malaria. How I know about her is from a documentary I watched during university, which told the story of how she and her team tested hundreds of herbal extracts, and how she even volunteered to take the first dose herself to prove the compound was safe. What she has achieved is staggering: her discovery is estimated to have saved millions of lives, and it earned her the Nobel Prize in 2015, making her the first Chinese woman to win a Nobel in science. Why I admire her is that she did her greatest work quietly, in an unglamorous laboratory, during difficult times, without seeking fame. She once said that she hoped young scientists would be patient and willing to work hard for many years without immediate results. In an age obsessed with quick fame, her patience and humility are rare and inspiring. So that's why Tu Youyou is the famous person I admire most.",
  "p2-celebrity-02": "I'd like to talk about an influencer I follow: Kurzgesagt, a German YouTube channel that creates beautiful animated videos explaining science and big questions. As for who this person is — well, it is actually a team, led by a creator named Philipp Dettmer, who narrates every video in a calm, warm voice. What kind of content they create is short animated documentaries on everything from black holes and vaccines to the Fermi paradox and why you should not be afraid of the future; each video takes months to research and animate, and it shows. How I found them was through a recommendation, seven years ago, when a friend sent me their video about the immune system, and I have been subscribed ever since. Why I enjoy following them is that they make complex science feel both understandable and awe-inspiring; their videos never talk down to you, and they are refreshingly honest about uncertainty. They also end every video with a reminder that the universe is vast, and that kindness is the only thing that makes sense. In a sea of shallow content, Kurzgesagt feels like a gift. So that's why following them is one of my favourite habits.",
  "p2-studyexperience-01": "I'd like to talk about a class I genuinely enjoyed: an art history elective I took in my second year of university. As for what the class was, it was a semester-long course called 'Understanding Western Painting', covering everything from Renaissance frescoes to modern abstract art, taught entirely through slides, museum visits, and endless discussion. When and where I took it was on Tuesday afternoons, in a small lecture hall with peeling paint and a temperamental projector, at my university in the south of China. What the teacher was like was a large part of why I loved it; Professor Liu was a tiny, energetic woman in her sixties who could talk for three hours about a single brushstroke and made the whole class feel like detectives examining clues. She would pause mid-lecture and ask, 'What do you think the painter wanted you to feel here?' and wait, genuinely curious, for our answers. Why I enjoyed this class so much is that it taught me to look slowly; before it, I saw paintings, and after it, I saw stories, secrets, and struggles. I have never become an art expert, but I now notice beauty everywhere, and I carry that habit with me daily. So that's why art history was the class I enjoyed most.",
  "p2-studyexperience-02": "I'd like to talk about a time I studied something genuinely difficult: I taught myself Japanese over the course of about a year. As for what I studied, it was the Japanese language — not just its grammar, but also three writing systems, including two thousand characters, most of which have multiple pronunciations. Why it was difficult is hard to exaggerate; the grammar works completely differently from my native language, the verb forms have endless variations, and reading a simple newspaper felt like deciphering a code. How I managed to learn it was through a stubborn combination of methods: I used an app for vocabulary every morning, watched anime with subtitles every evening, kept a notebook of grammar patterns, and, crucially, found a language exchange partner online who corrected my terrible sentences with remarkable patience. There were months when I made no visible progress, and I seriously considered giving up twice. How I felt after mastering it — well, I would not say I mastered it, but after a year I could hold a basic conversation and read a children's book cover to cover, and the moment I understood a full anime episode without subtitles, I screamed with joy. That experience taught me that difficulty is just a longer path, not a wall. So that's why studying Japanese was the hardest and most rewarding thing I have studied."
};
